"Wayne W. Scott" wrote:
> 
> Huh?  I thought they were way behind schedule.

   They are.  Even with the last announced schedule slip Intel
   was supposed to tape out in late June but didn't until late
   July. Also don't forget that the interval from first working
   silicon to shipping systems is often up to a year.  For
   example,  AMD had working silicon of K7 Athlon last fall.
   Merced will take months to verify and probably will require
   multiple spins to bring up AC test yields at the desired
   clock frequency (800 MHz ?) up to acceptable levels.  BTW,
   I'd love to know how fast merced was being clocked in the
   demo boxes shown off today.


> How does this fit in with the events of the past few days
> regarding the change of plans with W/NT & Alpha?

   Probably very little.  Merced prototype systems likely
   won't be available in large enough numbers for internal
   NT64 development at M$ for at least another quarter.
   Until then Alpha's carry the load.

   BTW, the Win2K demo on Merced shows that x86 compatibility
   mode is mostly working.  I wonder if the Linux demo was
   x86 based or IA-64 based.  If IA-64 isn't functional then
   Intel has created the world's hottest and most expensive
   Celereon class CPU.


All opinions strictly my own.
