Paul DeMone wrote:
>    Oh please, merced isn't the boogyman.  It is a bloated first cut at IA-64
>    implemented by a huge team of mostly rather inexperienced junior Intel
>    engineers.

I would have to disagree. For intel to have been able to transition its 8086
toy chip into a chip that is taken seriously as a competition to the alpha for
enterpsise solutions, one should not underestimate the power of Intel's engineers.

Remember that they are probably bound by much more demanding marketing forces
which are the cause for the poorer quality (remember the pentium bug ?). But
it doesn't mean that the engineers are incapable.

That merced will be inferior is no surprise. The requested specs are just too
complex to implement a high speed chip. Forcing the engineers to support IA64,
8086 as well as some HP instructions (is this correct) wresults in a much
bigger chip and definitely much more complex.

I'd say that the IA64 is probably going to much more advanced than an ALPHA.
It is just that its power in in being multiple cpu architectures bundled into
a chip, whereas ALPHA's force is focused on a very high performance single architecture.

Don'T underestimate the power of Intel. And what they don't invent themselves,
they inspire from others.



