I have specific concerns about VMS.  These concerns are not new.

*  Lack of Applications (up to date)

     This includes both commercial and freeware.   
     some examples 
     -   Latest version of Netscape Communicator  
        (the VMS version is outdated)
     -   Mail User Agents (anything but PINE is non-existant)
     -   lsf client 
         ( I know VMS has a better BATCH but this is what's being used)
     -    any scientific software
     -    A really robust TCPIP (sorry DEC TCPIP(aka UCX) still has problems)
     -    A CVS client

     In the freeware arena: the VMS community has an excellent track
     record of providing these applications (e.g. perl, ghostscript)
     However, I'd like to see more of this with perhaps
        some cooperation from Compaq to port these to VMS.
 
 * Targeting specific markets and forgetting all the others
   Our site is not one of the "targeted" markets. We believe we 
   are being pushed to "migrate".  Why?? VMS would support us much
   better than anything else (EXCEPT there are no applications).

 * A Marketing effort to show that VMS is alive and well for the future
   not just as a legacy OS.

 * Besides high availability etc VMS has many more features which are
   (or should be attractive) to many markets (e.g. resource management
    and control, a real working scheduler, a batch system, a good
    virtual memory system).   These should be advertised as advantages

 * The lack of desktop support (I know the VS10 is supposed to be
    the answer here  but 
       a) the memory prices are way out of wack for a workstation
       b) it is not considered a workstation for edu pricing and therefore
                 is more expensive than an XP1000

    When I asked why this workstation wasn't considered a
    "workstation" for edu pricing I was told this was the product manager's         decision.  )

Those are some of our concerns.

Feel free to contact me if anyone needs details

Rochelle Lauer
