In article <37CBDA6B.72D0EC8D@yale.edu>, 
    Rochelle Lauer <rochelle.lauer@yale.edu> writes:
> 
>      This includes both commercial and freeware.   
>      some examples 
>      -   Latest version of Netscape Communicator  
>         (the VMS version is outdated)

	Netscape 5 is supposed to be coming, hopefully fairly soon.

>      -   Mail User Agents (anything but PINE is non-existant)

	If you use PMDF you also get PMDF mail, which is a
	VMS Mail lookalike with extensions for MIME, etc. It's
	my impression that most people want PC based user agents
	these days. Around here mail messages are frequently
	MS Word attachments, so a user agent that doesn't have a reader
	for Word ( and Excel probably ) is of limited value.

>      -   lsf client 
>          ( I know VMS has a better BATCH but this is what's being used)
>      -    any scientific software

	I suspect this ( and the lack of much of the other freeware ) is
	a direct result of Digitals attempts to drive VMS out of the
	educational and low-end markets. Freeware tends to get developed
	by students and others with too much time on their hands, not by
	people working for the big banks :-)

>      -    A really robust TCPIP (sorry DEC TCPIP(aka UCX) still has problems)

	We use Multinet, no complaints with it. I understand the other
	TCP/IP stack from Process software isn't bad either.

>  
>  * Targeting specific markets and forgetting all the others
>    Our site is not one of the "targeted" markets. We believe we 
>    are being pushed to "migrate".  Why?? VMS would support us much
>    better than anything else (EXCEPT there are no applications).
> 

	I consider this to be their major marketing blunder. It seems to
	stem from a misguided belief that people driven off VMS will buy
	Unix or NT from Compaq instead ( though the killing of NT on
	Alpha can't have helped any in this respect ). If they ever
	realize that people driven off VMS become Sun or Dell customers
	instead they might wake up and change this approach.


> 
>  * Besides high availability etc VMS has many more features which are
>    (or should be attractive) to many markets (e.g. resource management
>     and control, a real working scheduler, a batch system, a good
>     virtual memory system).   These should be advertised as advantages
> 

	Don't want too many people buying VMS, might hurt those
	Proliant sales you know ;-)

>  * The lack of desktop support (I know the VS10 is supposed to be
>     the answer here  but 
>        a) the memory prices are way out of wack for a workstation

	Don't buy your memory from Compaq. The major 3rd party vendors
	have decent pricing for the DS10/VS10 ($1500US or less for
	512MB shouldn't be hard to find).

>        b) it is not considered a workstation for edu pricing and therefore
>                  is more expensive than an XP1000
> 
>     When I asked why this workstation wasn't considered a
>     "workstation" for edu pricing I was told this was the product manager's         decision.  )
> 

	Middle managers making bonehead decisions was always one of Digital's
	big problems. I remember being kept out of the CSLG program in
	Canada for a number of years. When I asked the program manager why
	small colleges weren't allowed in I was told "we have
	talked to our customers and they told us small colleges don't
	want the CSLG because it's too hard for them to administer "
	(it required an annual report listing the CPUs in the program and
	what software was installed on them).

	Of course, here in Canada education doesn't get special deals on
	any workstations so the above point is moot for us. 

