Alan Greig wrote:
> 
> David A Froble wrote:Since I am not bothered by admitting ignorance, I have to
> ask, what is/was
> 
> > MICA?  I've seen several references to it lately.
> >
> 
> MICA was the codename for the portable operating system being developed by Dave
> Cutler's team at Digital. By all accounts he took all the work straight over to
> Microsoft and  used it as the basis for NT. This is documented in Showstopper.


And FWIW, I have about 20 pages of MICA-related presentation material
from Charlie Matco's Unannounced Products File. MICA development started
around the time DEC began developing its PRISM RISC architecture. MICA
was to have been the VMS-like OS; a parallel effort was underway to
develop Prism/Ultix, a Unix port. MICA's SIL (system implementation
language) was PILLAR, presumably a follow-on to BLISS.

PRISM was scuttled in March 1989. As went PRISM, so went MICA. And so
went Cutler (and his MICA code) to Microsoft.

Terry Shannon



