My reading was that the only 64-bit development continuing on Alpha was that
directed at ensuring that the IA64 Win2K release would be as stable as
possible, not that 64-bit NT would ever be released on Alpha.

Furthermore, my reading was that this had been Compaq's plan for some time
now.

I've got to ask:  could the Register have been hoaxed?  I mean, I suggested
something like this, but only as the alternative to what I considered the
more likely possibility that there had simply been a major and to a large
degree unanticipated fuck-up by Compaq.  This says that the fuck-up was
carefully calculated.

If I were inclined toward Machiavellianism, I'd suggest that it *is* in fact
a hoax, perpetrated by Compaq on a Sunday (when no corporate response can be
expected) to guage customer reaction to taking a hard line and/or soften
customers up to make them eager to accept a slightly less hard-line position
tomorrow.  But while there's little reason for me to give Compaq much
benefit of the doubt, I just don't think they're that inventive (and I'm
also a bit reluctant to paint them with such a tenuous brush - they do a
more than adequate job of painting themselves, after all).

If it is not in fact a hoax, it does have the virtue of being relatively
unambiguous.  That, in turn, removes most inclinations I might have to
continue to try to read tea leaves in public, which should make at least
some people happy.

