Re the Register article, a few points.
1. Given the rumors I've been hearing about MS difficulties getting
W2000 going, availability of any 64 bit NT derivative anytime soon
could have been such vapor that the Q wanted no part of it.
2. While the guy writing the letters might want to educate
himself concerning Mica and similar subjects before asserting that
NT is wholly owned by Microsoft, it is not obvious why Compaq
should be involved save very peripherally in "productizing" any
NT release. If it's a Microsoft product, Microsoft is the outfit
that should productize it. That a third party decides to stop
giving freebie support for productizing would in normal cases
have no effect on whether the productization happened.
  Compaq has complained in the past about Microsoft's tendency
to push support expenses to OEMs (refusing to accept technical
calls from customers of its OSs when they were shipped with
machines) and refused to preload NT on some of its x86 boxes
in the past over that issue. If the OS belongs to Microsoft alone,
why should Compaq be paying to develop it or support it?

  The whole thread suggests that improper pressures were being
applied.

   With its acquisition of DEC, though, the Q gained some legal
ammo of potentially thermonuclear strength. It used to
appear to me they were the kind of outfit to use such. 

   If however 64 bit NT is total vapor and the next 32 bit NT
is having massive schedule/function problems, Alpha customers
(and everyone else) will do very well to look at DU, VMS,
OBSD, and Linux on the boxes for the next several years,
just to have something real and working. That Microsoft might
not just build NT/64 itself could well indicate simple
inability without extraordinary aid from the Q...aid which it
evidently didn't want to pay for or acknowledge.

How's that for a read?
