Actually its been a bit of a wierd week though on reflectionnot really that
unexpected.

I wonder what exitement those jolly chaps from the Q or should
it be the q have planned for you over then next couple of months ?

As to impaling on lances.

What Compaq have done makes sense.

CPQ had a possible window of opportunity to market a 64bit
NT solution on Alpha before Intel had a 64 bit platform to match.

What most people havn't picked up on is the fact that the window
has been closing not opening. True IA-64 has slipped and may
go on slipping, but Win2000 AKA NT5 which Win64/NT/Alpha
would have been based on has also been slipping just as
fast if not faster. At the same time win2000 beta test reports
have been pretty dire causing Gartner and other analysts
to advise caution when deploying Win2000 for production
mission critical environments. Gartner are advising people
to wait until at least the 2and service pack.

So CPQ were in the position of having a shrinking window
of opportunity to market Win64 with customers in their
target market (reliable, high performance servers) being
advised to wait until SP2. This put actual large scale
Win64 deployments easily into Merced time frames.

CPQ were also going to need to do a lot of work with
ISV's for 64 bit versions and could easily have kick
started the 64bit NT market only to have Intel take
all the fruits of their labours.

I am only suprised it took them so long to take an
axe to both win32 and win64 on Alpha neither of
which in my opinion where anything other than a
pottential embarassment to them.

