Dave Cherkus <cherkus@unimaster.com> wrote in message
news:37c6e316@News.Destek.net...

...

> Just to judge your perpsective, are you one of the ones being screwed
> here?

No, I'm not.  I'm nothing more than someone who used to love DEC and have a
great deal of respect for Tandem, and if Compaq is going to ride them into
the ground, I'd like to see them held accountable.  Better yet, though, I'd
like to see enough customers get sufficiently upset to make Compaq
reevaluate its entire strategy - but I'm not holding my breath.

Compaq certainly didn't start DEC's problems.  Ken Olsen lost a good deal of
control (or his sense of direction, to the degree that he still was in
control) by the late '80s, and Palmer dramatically worsened things during
his reign.  But Compaq rode in with definite pretensions of being a White
Knight.

Some of the press have portrayed Pfeiffer as an Alpha visionary.  I haven't
a clue whether he really was, and was foiled by other elements of Compaq, or
whether he just didn't understand any more than Palmer did (but talked a
better line).  The possibility of combining the best elements of Tandem and
DEC technology into a team that could have had a decent chance of halting
the NT march into the mid-range-and-up market certainly existed, though, and
Compaq did virtually nothing to bring it to fruition.

So I blame Compaq for actively raising up and now dashing the hopes of
long-suffering customers who deserve better - certainly those customers who
committed to NT on Alpha, but in a larger sense much of the rest of the DEC
customer base as well.  And I see absolutely no reason why they should cut
Compaq any more slack.

I won't bother responding to the rest:  people are going to form their own
opinions of what constitutes critical mass for the Alpha platform in making
continued use of Tru64 and OVMS practical, as well of how committed Compaq
may be to those systems over time.

- bill






