One can't help but wonder...

In face of a seemingly insane move, does Compaq possibly have it's
largest, most powerful Linux-Alpha torpedoes aimed squarely at
Micro$haft's keel?

Think about it...

Micro$haft controls the desktop. Therefore, if you want to be on the
desktop, you dance to Micro$haft's tune.

Compaq has been losing ground in the Intel desktop market, no doubt in
the server segment as well, mostly due to pricing (there's that word
again!).

Alpha is probably viewed as a drain on ProLiant market potential.
Therefore, kill off the Alpha threat by killing off W/NT-Alpha.

Along that same line of thinking, Alpha workstations may have been
viewed as a drain on the DeskPro market. Again, kill off the Alpha
threat by killing off W/NT-Alpha.

Alpha holds greater promise than the as yet unrealized IA64, yet Compaq
chooses the "devils it knows" (IA32, Emperor Wm-I). Sort of makes you
wonder whether the next Einstein was aborted today.

Seem suicidal to you?

Yeah, to me, too. So, I gotta believe that the "Q" have Linux-Alpha
cards they're not showing.

...or is this maybe a prelude to a revival of the often dreamed of,
never (yet) realized OpenVMS-Intel?

Think about THAT for a moment...

If Compaq drops the Alpha Linux ball as they have the OVMS and Tru64
balls (their record speaks for itself), then Alpha is doomed.

If Compaq remains strong in the IA32 market, however, there'll be no
choice but to step backwards to 32-bit VMS on IA32 if Alpha indeed does
die, unless OpenVMS dies with it, or by some miracle, IA64 finally makes
its debut and then succeeds beyond even Alpha's most far-fetched
promises, and eventually receives an OpenVMS port (OpenVMS-IA64 - not
sure I like that).

--
David J. Dachtera
dba DJE Systems
