David A Froble  <davef@tsoft-inc.com> wrote:
>If it's only new development that is halted, then what's the issue?  Current
>users who are happy with their systems will be supported.  It'll do tomorrow
>what it does today.  I'd guess support would include fixing problems, so if
>a problem occurs, it'll be fixed, and the system will continue doing tomorrow
>what it does today.
IMHO the big problem with this reasoning is that unlike more mature
operating systems, NT is often sold on the promises of features coming
in the future, and a fear that it will take over the world in the future.
So, companies bought NT 4 and created an environment which may not have
done everything they wanted, yet they bought the promises that the
Windows Formerly Known as Windows NT 5.0 (Windows 2000) would make that
investment pay off.  Instead, they are now faced with the reality that
their strategy will require a shift to x86-based systems they may have
rejected as inferior technology, or a rehosting of their environment on
a non-Microsoft operating system.  This provides at least one clear case
where many sites would feel betrayed, cheated, etc.
