I wouldn't hold my breath for new Alpha initiatives, after seeing Compaq
eliminate not only 32-bit NT support but future 64-bit NT support at a time
when Alpha enjoys the best potential lead (both in performance and in
maturity of 64-bit NT development) over IA64 it is ever likely to have.

Compaq's only interest in continuing to work with MS on 64-bit NT on Alpha
seems to be to make NT as ready as possible to move to Merced, rather to try
to provide a real competitor to IA64 and in so doing bolster Alpha industry
penetration in general (after all, while there may not be too many reasons
to run 32-bit NT on Alphas, one would expect the availability of 64-bit NT
on Alpha to be at least somewhat more attractive).

I've suggested before that Compaq has no interest in being a software
company, save insofar as it can use its existing software acquisitions to
increase current revenue.  This just shows that it has equally little
interest in being a processor designer/provider.  Remember that historically
Compaq has never *created* anything, it just built stuff out of the
creations of others - and seems to be in the process of retreating back to
that status.

