There was a fuse in some Alpha processors which,if blown, would
disable virtual memory modes which VMS and Unix use but allow
NT (and I believe Linux) still to work. However that was never
done on ANY Alphas. Instead some were shipped with firmware
that didn't support VMS (well, didn't support it easily at
any rate). Of course, for VMS to run on something there have
to be CPU routines for the box type. Those aren't huge but are
very machine specific. As I recall there are boot and run versions
of them that have to be in place.

One of the better things for Compaq to think about is whether
the Dec-Microsoft agreement was breached. Recall there were
some lawsuit issues where DEC refrained from suing Microsoft
over some matters of DEC code (from Mica I believe) and some
DEC patents, there being some quid pro quo. If Microsoft
breached that agreement, Compaq, as the successor company, may
have the rights to sue to have NT taken off the market, or to
demand royalties (and a most-favored-company treatment) for NT,
with hopefully very complete public disclosure of ALL the
particulars. (I've heard the business about the code from sources
I'm inclined to believe, but it is officially rumor.)

Under current circumstances, Microsoft would be in a world of hurt
if they had to rewrite the NT kernel; it'd be like trying to
replace the foundation of a skyscraper without cracking walls.
Nor would full disclosure of the facts to the entire world
likely do them any good at all. If they're in breach and Compaq
dislikes it, the Q have a powerful lever.

There were words about sharing NT internal info with VMS way
back when, of course, which far as I know did not get translated
into action. Could be a breach exists already.

However, NT customers would do best to move over to VMS, where
DBMSs can be 64 bits wide and run hundreds of times faster than
32 bit DBMSs. If NT innards look familiar to us VMS folks, the
NT folks will find VMS innards have a very familiar look too.


