In article <7q1lm8$lev$3@uni00nw.unity.ncsu.edu>, BSD Bob <bsdbob@weedcon1.cropsci.ncsu.edu> writes:
|> I have picked up several DS3100's and would like to get one running
|> on NetBSD-1.4.1 pmax.  I did pick up a TK50 tape drive, also.
|> 
|> 1.   Can a TK50 bootable tape be written on something like a Sun3 or
|>      Sun4 or PC BSD flavor machine and then set up to boot on the
|>      DS3100?  Logically, I would expect it to be possible, but I
|>      was wondering if there were any gotchas the DEC folks might
|>      be aware of in mixing hardware.
|> 
|> 2.   Can a non-DEC scsi tape such as an Archive or Wangtek 60mb
|>      1/4 inch cartridge streamer be used in a temporary fashion
|>      to boot a DS3100?  (Yeah, I know, that is heresy... but....)
|> 
|> I could net boot, but the machine is spread out all over the
|> workbench, not near the home net.  If it is possible to boot it
|> standalone from non-DEC tapes, that would be the easiest mode.
|> Writing the TK50 tape would be the next-easiest mode.  The only
|> other think I can think of would be to dd the fs onto the raw
|> drive (I tried that and it did not work too well from sun boxes).

hi Bob,

Netbsd/pmax doesn't include boot-blocks for tapes.  If you had
ever dealt with TK-50s you would understand why.  Netbooting is
the preferred solution, if you can manage it.

Otherwise, dd'ing the fs image onto the raw drive will work.
Just make sure that you use whatever incantations are needed
to let the Sun over-write its own disklabel area with the diskimage.
If the 3100 has a floppy (or you have a SCSI floppy) Simon Burge could
probably kludge one up for you.


--Jonathan Stone (pure personal opinion: not speaking for Compaq,
		  or NetBSD for that matter).



