> > I'd say things have changed. In a Microsoft article someone else posted
> > the URL for, the last two paragraphs say that Microsoft and Compaq still
> > has a "strong relationship", and then slams Compaq by saying NT x86 SMP
> > systems have better performance than Tru64 UNIX Alpha SMP. Grrrrr...
>
> Yeah, I noticed that. I believe Microsoft actually said
> "price-performance", but note that they are comparing NT on Intel with
> Tru64 on Alpha. Apples to oranges, but such is the bitter fruit of
> benchmarketing...
>

Actually the 8 CPU Xeons are faster and cheaper as are the 4 CPU
Xeons when compared with a 4x 21264 Alphaserver. So they are
not claiming just price performance but also performance. There is
however some devil in the detail.

The NT/4-8 CPU Intel TPC-C's are all published using MS SQL-Server
which is quite good at doing something as simple as TPC-C but
pretty deficient in other respects.

The AlphaServer/Tru64 results use Sybase, a much more capable
DBMS than SQL-Server but with a bigger footprint. There have
been SQL-Server and Sybase TPC-C results published for the
same hardware in the past which have indicated that the additional
Sybase footprint results in a ~30% performance hit.

That said the Tru64 TPC-C results for all the new Alpha servers
have been poor given the SPECint performance of the CPU's
used in the boxes, much worse than the expected 30% hit
for using a real DBMS rather than SQL-Server.

Regards
Andrew Harrison
Enterprise IT Architect






