"Kevin P. Fleming" wrote:
> 
> We chose our AlphaServer 4000 in December of 1996 for the express purpose of
> running NT 4.0 (which was brand-new at the time) to be the main server for
> our (admittedly small) enterprise. The biggest reason we chose the
> AlphaServer was not so much the Alpha vs. Pentium speed issues (although
> that was present as well), but the fact that, at the time, there was no
> comparable Intel-based system in terms of hardware expandability (beyond two
> CPUs, 2G or more of RAM, three power supplies, etc.). Everything available
> then would have been maxed out at two Pentium IIs, with no upward growth.
> Interesting to note that it's taken nearly three years for the Intel-based
> systems to reach the point where they could actually compete with my
> three-year old machine.
> 
> That said, I've been happy with the system, although its performance is
> lagging now since we've not upgraded beyond the original 5/300 CPU. As I
> look around for CPU upgrades, I find that I could buy a dual-processor Xeon
> machine for about the same cost as a CPU upgrade to a 533 MHz EV56 to my
> existing machine. Now that the NT-on-Alpha situation is up in the air, I'm
> really thinking it might be time to switch platforms. I don't want to, the
> machine has been great, but I can't see the benefit of sticking with a
> platform that, at any time (as Terry Shannon already said), could just be
> dropped without warning.

Yep. It could. But I don't want to be accused of spreading false (as
opposed to TRUE) rumours. Best thing to do right now is wait a few days
for the Q to come out with a detailed statement of intentions and
directions.

The decision to decommit from versions of Win32-on-Alpha past SP6 was,
IMHO, a business decision based on Economics 101. And from what I've
seen re: shipments and revenues, the decision made good economic sense.
The sad irony is that one key reason for the lackluster adoption of
Win32-on-Alpha was the utterly squatulent marketing of same by DEC, and
subsequently, the Q. Seems to me that the Novas and Aarons (and, from
time to time, the Terrys) of this world were stronger proponents of
Alpha and NT than were the aforementioned vendors.

Whatever, the ball is now in the Q's court. I expect that news will be
forthcoming from this source Real Soon Now.

Cheers,

Terry Shannon
http://www.acersoft.com



