Should Compaq elect to offer attractive financial incentives to Alpha NT
customers who want to move up to a REAL OS (Tru64 UNIX or OpenVMS), the
message would be much more effective.

Alpha NT customers have several choices...

1) Hope that Win64 on Alpha will actually materialize.

2) Migrate to OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX on Alpha.

3) Migrate to a ProLiant or competitive IA-32 platform and continue to
run NT.

Option 1 demands a hell of a lot of optimism.

Option 2 would require Compaq to bite the bullet on OS license charges.
IMHO it would be worth sinking the incisors into the lead just to retain
Alpha customers. The downside to this option is that lobotomized NT-only
Alpha boxes cannot run OpenVMS or Tru64 UNIX.

Option 3 would not be good news for Compaq. Once a customer is off
Alpha, there is precious little reason to remain a loyal Compaq
customer. Dell, IBM, HP, et al, all offer decent IA-32 solutions.

The Q clearly has some "issues to work through" here.

