I'm only relying on my memory here (I used to work for the DEC PC Business
Unit at the time of the DECpc XL introduction), but the there were several
revision of the so called Intel "Neptune" system chipset the machine was
based on.

I think three of the chips (in a set of four) were on the CPU daughtercard
and one on the motherboard.

Initial revision (I'm afraid I don't remember the revs) chipsets had no SMP
(Intel MPS 1.x spec) support, but newer ones did. And mixing never rev
Neptune chips on the daughtercard with older on the motherboard didn't
work well (or at all).

Those guaranteed to have the correct rev Neptune chips were those sold as
dual-CPU systems from the factory, but DEC also had an upgrade package that
included a new rev motherboard and CPU daughtercard (field installable) and
another upgrade for machines with the correct chipset in place (no new
motherboard required, customer installable, included only daughtercard with
new CPUs).

I also remember running CPUs of different stepping levels, and the
first/primary CPU - the socket that was used when there was only one
processor - had to hold the older stepping CPU, and the secondary the newer
stepping level CPU. I tried to swap them, but the system refused to boot if
the newer CPU (in my case one with the floating point fix whereas the older
one had the bug) was in the first socket.

So, in other words, depending on the Neptune chipset revision (and thus the
motherboard & CPU daughtercard revision, since the chips are soldered) your
machine may operate with dual CPUs or it simply might not.

Since I don't remember the revision numbers, you will find out by trying and
if if works then fine, otherwise, too bad and can't be fixed without
upgrading the motherboard to a newer revision and most likely also the CPU
daughtercard needs to be upgraded to the same level.

FWIW,
 ...petri backstrom
    Microsoft Finland


