In article <taterskins-ya023480001908992340420001@news.patriot.net>, taterskins@patriot.net (Ramon L. Tate) writes:
>We seem to be confusing "marketing" with "advertising" in this (and other
>similar) discussions here. While the distinction is one that a first-year
>MBA student learns, many people don't really understand or appreciate the
>difference. A pretty good explanation of this can be found in "Crossing the
>Chasm" by Geoffrey Marsh, a book I would recommend to all IT professionals
>anyway. Basically, marketing is mostly finding the *right* place for your
>product and then creating irresistible reasons for the targeted customers
>to but it. What you are actually doing is creating a "whole product", one
>that contains all the pieces needed to do the job. Advertising *is* a part
>of this, but only one part; if you don't have all the other pieces,
>advertising will be a waste of money.

By that definition Digital and Compaq were/are masters of "marketing"
OpenVMS, since they specify the places it should be used right down to the
industry and waste no funds at all promoting the product outside of those
target markets.  However, this level of targetting is too "precise" for
what is only a very good, very reliable computer operating system. 
Virtually all industries can use such a product and the only reason to
restrict the target to such extreme reliability critical market niches is
if the intent is to impose monopolistic type prices on those customers who
have little choice in operating  systems, while driving away all other
possible customers who do have a choice. 

