Hmmm (why do I keep saying that?).

"It is clear that our ProLiant products and capabilities meet the growing
requirement for 32-bit Windows NT solutions that deliver performance,
scalability, manageability and reliability. This enables us to take actions
that will simplify our platform strategy and our value proposition for
customers and software partners."

On the face of it, a pretty reasonable statement, though some posters here
would apparently disagree that Compaq Intel-based servers are as reliable as
their previous DEC Intel-based counterparts, let alone Alpha platforms.

Of course, it does leave those NT on Alpha customers (12% of the Alpha
base?) who had planned to upgrade to Win2K in the lurch - and without a
particularly generous amount of warning.  But hey, they've lived with NT 4
for so long already they may not even notice.

"We have already announced an aggressive plan to grow Tru64 UNIX on Alpha in
such key markets as high performance technical computing, eCommerce,
telecommunications and enterprise applications, among others. We will
continue to service and maintain the highest levels of   customer
satisfaction with our OpenVMS customers."

Just one more example of a very real difference in corporate perception and
plans between Tru64 and OVMS.

"During the past few years a number of customers have made a commitment to
Windows NT on Alpha. We are completely focused on protecting their
investments and keeping them as satisfied Compaq customers. We will support
32-bit Windows NT on Alpha for as long as they require and offer migration
paths to other Compaq platforms."

This sounds so much like so many statements made about OVMS that it's almost
eerie.  It makes it just that much harder to believe that Compaq is any more
committed to aggressive pursuit of OVMS markets than it is to 32-bit
NT-on-Alpha markets:  the only problem is that there's not (yet) something
that Compaq perceives as being a suitable substitute for OVMS in Compaq's
repertoire.

"A simplified platform strategy will make it easier for customers and
partners to understand the value we deliver."

Those darn customers are just *so* easily confused that we shouldn't give
them any choices we don't have to...

"It will also enable us to maximize our investments in strategic products
and focused markets that will drive market leadership and profitable
growth."

... and we're really not a big enough corporation to devote resources to
anything but the most short-term-profitable areas.

"We will continue to invest in Alpha as a core component of our NonStop
eBusiness strategy, including next generation Alpha chip technology and a
robust Alpha systems road map."

No commitment to a generation beyond that one though:  with luck, we'll be
able to migrate anything we care about to McKinley.

I think I'm beginning to get the hang of this translation stuff - just takes
a while to get past the inclination to give corporate honchos credit for at
least some degree of integrity.

- bill





