In article <gyjw3.3767$iE.104304@stones>, "Mark Lamond" <markl@markl.force9.net> writes:
|> 
|> Does anybody have any details on where i can get pinouts of the various
|> ports, so i can make up some custom cables. I assume that the keyboard is a
|> special type specifically for DEC equipment?

Both the 5000/200 and the 3000/600 expect a DEC LK-201 or compatible (e.g.,
LK-401) keyboard and a DEC mouse. Both speak rs-232 at 4800 baud; I forget
the parity requirements.  Commodity PC parts won't work.

The DECstations have a 15-pin D-shell connector which has two serial
channels for mouse and keyboard; there's a standard DEC cable that connects
to a DIN-style mouse serial cable and a DEC (micro-mmj) LK-xxx keyboard. I
beleive the pinout for these is in the reference material to which pointers
have already been posted; AFAIK the 3000/600 uses the same cable.


|> Do you know if there is any way a standard VGA monitor can be used on this
|> machine. 

[...]

|> which makes me wonder where the monitor get`s it's sync signals from, unless
|> they are somehow generated inside the monitor?

The TC video cards do sync on green. You need either a monitor that can
handle synch-on-green directly, or an external box to extract the sync signal.


|> Any more information on the monitor and keyboard would be useful, as until i
|> get these two items the machine is basically useless.

At least on the 5000/200, you can pull the frame buffer and connect a
serial console (9600, 8 bits no parity). Some combination of a loopback
mouse connector and/or pulling the video card should work on the 3000/600.
(In a pinch, a paperclip will do; it's an FAQ on the NetBSD/pmax pages and
in the VMS FAQ for 3100s; but it assumes you already have a cable that
converts from the DB-15 to the round mouse pinout.)


Jonathan Stone -- personal opinion only, not speaking for COMPAQ.



