> >We seem to be confusing "marketing" with "advertising" in this (and other
> >similar) discussions here. While the distinction is one that a first-year
> >MBA student learns, many people don't really understand or appreciate the
> >difference. A pretty good explanation of this can be found in "Crossing the
> >Chasm" by Geoffrey Marsh, a book I would recommend to all IT professionals
> >anyway. Basically, marketing is mostly finding the *right* place for your
> >product and then creating irresistible reasons for the targeted customers
> >to but it. What you are actually doing is creating a "whole product", one
> >that contains all the pieces needed to do the job. Advertising *is* a part
> >of this, but only one part; if you don't have all the other pieces,
> >advertising will be a waste of money.

To which David Mathog (mathog@seqaxp.bio.caltech.edu) replied:
> By that definition Digital and Compaq were/are masters of "marketing"
> OpenVMS, since they specify the places it should be used right down to the
> industry and waste no funds at all promoting the product outside of those
> target markets.  However, this level of targetting is too "precise" for
> what is only a very good, very reliable computer operating system. 
> Virtually all industries can use such a product and the only reason to
> restrict the target to such extreme reliability critical market niches is
> if the intent is to impose monopolistic type prices on those customers who
> have little choice in operating  systems, while driving away all other
> possible customers who do have a choice. 
> 
> Regards,
etc.

For the "rest of the story", get a copy of "Inside the Tornado", the
successor to "Crossing the Chasm". While Moore's writings may not be
pioneering, they do succinctly and readably present the basic dynamics at
work in the hi-tech marketplace. What Digital failed to do was what most
hi-tech firms fail to do: take their products from a "niche" market to the
position of market leadership. If this just isn't possible (and it probably
wasn't for lots of reasons), then you have to accept that and work hard at
expanding and securing the niches into which your product offering(s) fit.

For all the reasons that have been aired ad nauseum on this news group over
the past 10 years, this was precisely what Digital failed to do. The niches
have shrunken in size and number, and the product (primarily VMS, but
actually the combination of OS and platform) has become associated with
only "high end", "we'll pay anything to keep it going" kinds of markets.
And for a lot of other reasons previously well-stated here, this isn't
really a sustainable situation in the long run.

So, the bottom line, as I see it, is precisely what other in this forum
have stated before: Compaq is going to have to ACTIVELY expand VMS/Alpha to
new niches and expand downward to lower-cost development/training level
systems. IF they can be vigorous enough about this, they MIGHT be able to
pull it out of the fire. If not, the current attrition will continue to
some point of no return, at which time Compaq will either have to shoot it
in the head or sell if off at fire-sale prices - CA/VMS anyone?

