It certainly makes sense that the Athlon would go looking for BIOS since
it did spring from Intel lineage.  The more fundamental problem that I
was hoping to hear a solution for starts with SROM.  Alphas start in a
serial fashion whereas x86s start in a parallel fashion.  Alphas need a
serial stream of configuration data before they can hope to begin
loading console firmware from Flash.

One could flash in x86 code to run a board if one could get an x86 to
open up the SROM serial port.  If the Athlon is (capable :-) of starting
up by reading an SROM, one would only need to recompile the SROM codes
to x86 in order to start the board down the road toward replacing the
Alpha firmware with BIOS.

However, due to lack of replies from elsewhere, I'm going to have to
assume that this solution does not, and probably will not exist.
Perchance the best bet is a card redesign with a toggle switch between
two boot methods.  Provided there is enough market momentum behind
figuring out how to do this at all.

One of the big propaganda pieces being waved about regarding the K7 is
that it is pin compatible with EV6.  This is kindof goofy if you ask me,
you could manufacture a 21064 in EV6 process.  Don't ask me why you
would do this, just that being pin compatible with a process length
doesn't make much sense.  Being pin compatible with a processor family
makes more sense.  So, to rephrase the original question, is the K7 pin
compatible with the 21264?  Would the K7 run in a Psunami chip set based
system, provided one took the time to design the correct reset circuit?
Does anyone know of plans to release a board which would be smart enough
to run either processor?


"Ray T." wrote:

> The BIOS would not work since it is written with Alpha
> op codes.
>
> Ray T.




