: make Intel's job very hard with its very late merced/ia64/whatever-this-month's-name-is.

Doubtful.

If I can give Compaq credit for one thing, they've probably been the most
aggressive Top-10 PeeCee vendor at utilizing alternative processors like
AMDs and for a while the Cyrix MediaGX.  Granted, these were/are in units
geared towards the home market, but that's where Intel has felt the most
pressure from AMD.

In fact, IIRC, Compaq was the first traditional PeeCee manufacturer to use
AMD processors back when AMD first came out with their 486 clones (maybe
even earlier?).

I don't think there's much love lost between Compaq & Intel, just necessity
of business.  That said, I can still completely understand Compaq's
position on Alpha-NT

1) Stop playing lapdog to M$ - Why should Compaq foot the bill for Alpha NT
development for a multi-billion dollar company?  I can imagine to a large,
formerly hardware-only company the industry tradition of the platform vendor
footing the development bills for the ISVs seems a bit odd, especially with
an ever-decreasing bottom-line.  

2) Why support an interim strategy when it appears the 64-bit strategy is
close at hand anyways?  64-bit NT has been demoed several times on Alpha, so
effort would be better spent in polishing up that instead of keeping people
employed pushing something that doesn't even differentiate well from it's
Intel sibling (Win2k-32bit).  64-bit NT is a real difference to be offered,
and would be more beneficial to Compaq than a 32-bit hybrid like they've
been pushing.  While I'm no big NT fan in the first place, it seems to me
there would be a much more convincing argument for Alpha NT if it offered
something more than Intel NT (i.e. 64-bit way ahead of Merced)...

BTW, that's Merced pronounced "Murk-y-d" or "Murky-d" :)

