>This is a joke, right?  Or a hoax?  I would think Compaq has quite a few
>Alpha-experienced software types left over from the now defunct NT
>group.

The NT group is in Washington state.  While we have some file system folks
out there, there are advantages to here.  "Alpha-experienced" is
really far less important than "UNIX internals experience" as we do much
more work at all levels inside the kernel than is needed for NT on
Alpha.  Except for the small percentage of assembler code, Alpha
knowledge that affects all kernel developers is tied more to coaxing
efficient cache use than to Alpha-specific skills.  (And that applies
directly to Merced or any other modern Killer Micro.)

>Sick humor or piss poor timing.  Hmm, wonder if they offer employment
>contracts, job security being non-existent at Compaq these days.

I worked at DEC from 1974-1978, several other places until 1993, and
back here since then.  There is far more job security here than I had
anywhere else.  Note that this is a better time to be looking for
work than it was in 1992, when I went through Chapter 11 for the second
time.  (Personally, I think everyone should experience Chapter 11 at
a small-medium size company once, but once is enough!)

There's a very good chance that NT on Alpha folks don't want to work
on UNIX, but I know none of the details of how that is being handled.
I don't even know how many engineers are affected.
