I personally do not see the end of development on 32 bit NT to be a
problem.  You'll note that the current stuff and customers will continue
to be supported.  (Damn, I love to see the old VMS treatment applied to
NT.)  The idiots in this world will continue to buy Intel, figuring
that if it's good enough for composing a letter, than it's good enough
to forcast the whether, store all of an enterprise's historical data,
and whatever else important that needs done.  They'll also put up with
BSODs, data corruption, frequent OS re-installs, flaky behavior, and all
the other problems that god Bill has convinced them is normal.

If NT on Alpha has any future, it's the 64 bit version, even if only
because it's something else to wait on.  You'll notice that development
for this was not stopped.  With the advent of 64 bit NT, the 32 bit version
is at the end of it's development life in any case.  Since Alpha is 64 bit,
why should Compaq develope for a platform they do not sell?

Dave

