But let me raise a thought.

First, let me state up front, I'm no NT'er. I use it but have neither
a disdain for it, nor revel in the glories of its possibilities.

But it occurs to me  that no matter what amount of effort
(engineering or marketing) that Compaq did for Alpha/NT -
if Microsoft didn't really endorse it in a big way and throughout
their product line - Alpha/NT was destined for only marginal use.

In doing this, Compaq has thrown down the gauntlet to Microsoft.
If Microsoft really wants to move into the back room and prove
they are ready for real enterprise computing with a serious
processor behind it, they (Microsoft) need to pick up the
engineering and marketing of NT on Alpha.

If Alpha is important to Microsoft, the cost to them to pick
up the engineering is minuscule. If it's not important to Microsoft,
then there was nothing Compaq could  have ever done to really
turn the tide.

Thinking positively, there could be no better pitch for the Alpha
(and Compaq would gladly push them out the door at a record pace)
than if Microsoft themselves announced Alpha/NT as their supported
high end enterprise platform.

- joe

