I like this statement:

     We want to keep our OpenVMS customers as Compaq
     customers. That means we want to make sure that any
     customer who decides to transition from OpenVMS makes that
     transition to another Compaq product. 

In the old DEC days, customers were forced to leave VMS by 3rd party s/w
houses and mostly by DEC (marketing/sales/3rd-party-support) itself. And
that always meant to switch away from DEC in any way. DEC didn't want to
see it (or wanted to die ?) and continued their VMS kastratation. RIP

CPQ seems at least to want them to stay Q customers...

As we ditched COMPAQ at our site (because of many PC problems years before
we started to ditch DEC (ULTRIX is gone, dUNIX is gone, VMS is dying), the
strategy to keep us as a customer is still unknown to me (U**X is now only
SOLARIS, HPUX and AIX, PC is HP WINTEL Server, Noname Clients). I doubt.

And we have a lot of work for Q's machines, but decisions are no longer
based on tech fact sheets only. DEC/Q lost before the fact sheet starts.
It's a said story...

btw: I haven't seen a DEC or a Q salesmen the last three or four years.
So why will the message come to the bosses? They don't read WWW, they don't
attend DECUS or COMPAQEVENTS. Maybe they don't even heard of the Alphas
(we have Alphas since 1993). They always refer VMS as "VAX". No wonder...

