On Thu, 19 Aug 1999 16:17:33 -0400, JF Mezei
<jfmezei.spamnot@videotron.ca> wrote:

>OK, I read some more.
>We will continue to focus on solutions for customers in select
>        target markets. 
>        Outside of these target markets, we will provide an easy
>        migration path to Compaq Tru64 UNIX and Windows NT for
>        those customers who want to migrate. This supports our goal
>        of keeping OpenVMS customers as Compaq customers. 
>The 4 letter word would no longer fit on a letter-sized paper because it would
>be so big.
>
>How can a company be SO STUPID to say such a thing ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? 

Me when i read these remarks, inre migration, i tend to think to 
they're directed at shops, who in the mid 80's/early 90's bought 
1-2 Microvaxes to use as small print/file/office-app servers, or 
to run a *small* number (or small in scope), business applications
Shops that could 'seemingly' more cheaply use using NTservers.

the gist i came away w/ from presentations by Rich Marcello at DECUS
Providence,  was that they considered the 'target' VMS markets to be:

	+ Healthcare
             + Manufacturing
             + Telecommunications
             + Finance (capital markets, ie 
                Stock Exchanges and related industries)
             + Public sector
ie,
http://www.openvms.digital.com/presentations/openvmsbusiness/sld014.htm

on the one hand, i applaud the apparent focus (ie, there's actually  
some substance here, ie, w/o mumbling inre migrations to 
'Industry Standard' platforms ...).

on the the other hand, it was hard to discern whether expanding this 
list is/was out of the question.

f'instance, with a few (additional) key applications, proxy-servers,
firewalls,  VMS would make an excellent choice for systems to put
on the outer edges of a network, exposed to the public internet.

as such, VMS could be targeted at ISP's, and as part of whole soln
for eBusiness'es.   Rather than leaving a customer to deploy some
other platform, on the publically exposed edges of a network.

more generally, w/ all the CPQ layoffs of Digital Classic employees,
I wonder how much *new* development or funding we might see on the
various layered products.   

f'instance, i've always heard rumors on  && off, that a buyout/buyback
of the former PolyCenter suites (from CA) was being considered.

it doesn't concern me particularly,  (we use almost no 3rd party s/w)
but with the trends (back && forth, it seems) in the industry inre 
all-encompassing, shrink-wrapped solns, I'm  sure it concerns others.




