In article <37B9BEB4.856C401B@as.lmco.com>, Kevin Angelone <kevin.angelone@as.lmco.com> writes:
:If you have example C code or more information about a simple router like the
:one you mentioned, it would be a great help.  Thank you for your time and
:thorough response, you've already been helpful.

  I wish it were going to be as easy as that.  DECnet and TCP/IP are 
  rather different protocols, with different assumptions...  (More
  than a few folks have tried tunneling, and it gets "interesting"...)

  The simple cases -- transparent task-to-task -- will probably pass 
  through a home-grown "tunnel" (DECnet->IP->DECnet) or "translator" 
  (DECnet->IP) with minimal effort.  Applications that use out-of-band 
  or similar might be a little more interesting.

  If you create your own "tunnel" or "translator", you'll probably end 
  up dealing -- at least initially -- with each DECnet application you
  are using separately...  Or switching to DCL tools such as COPY/FTP,
  which use protocols IP directly, etc.

  As a starting point, you'll end up working with the existing examples 
  available in UCX$EXAMPLES: and in SYS$EXAMPLES:DB*.*.

  DECnet-Plus over IP would be FAR easier...  (You might be best served
  with a pair of intervening DECnet-Plus nodes using IP, with DECnet
  Phase IV routing used on either end of the connection -- this would
  work much like a tunnel...)

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




