though I
>>> will never use it?


Please check with the Q for the current terms and conditions for their
licensing.  Also non-Q layered products may be licensed differently.

Of course, be aware that every person I have ever talked to from the Q has
given me a slightly different interpretation of their policies.  Get the
stuff in writing, and then review it yourself, and possibly with paid legal
help, if needed.

I do not know if the license conditions are on line, but they were in the
last version of a software catalog that I looked at.  A contract between
Digital/Compaq and your or your client's company can also superceed the
standard terms and conditions.


In general, the Operating System license is tied to a specific machine.  I
have seen interpretations by the Q that SIP type keys like VMS Clustering
and DECNET are or are not.  The layered product class F type of things like
FORTRAN, can be transfered from one machine to another as long as they use
the same amount of units.


IIRC: All of the Q's licensing policies allow the temporary running of their
software on a replacement machine while the primary is broken.  However, the
terms never say anything about testing that configuration before hand.  You
may wish to check with the Q directly, and in particular the loaner products
division, that can if they want, issue you FREE temporary keys for the
reasons that you want that are fully legal to use.

Also be aware that the license KEY is not a software license, and is not
proof that you have a software license.  It says so right on it.  However do
not lose it, as the DEC # on it somewhere is the only way that I have ever
been able to get the Q to work a license transfer, or fix a licensing key
problem.


In many cases, it is not necessary to have backup hardware that is identical
to the production hardware with VMS.  In this case, the customers original
license keys may not work in all cases.

Also, older equipment was licensed differently than the present.  A FORTRAN
license purchased for a VAXserver 3500 back when, would only have 10 units,
and can only be   A FORTRAN license purchased for the same machine today
would have 200 units.

It seems that when you are purchasing NEW hardware from the Q, that is the
best time to make any licensing adjustments.  For example, the a unit
FORTRAN license can be converted to a 200 at no charge, if you happen to be
purchasing a system with a Workgroup class designation.  If you are getting
a higher class of machine than what your licenses are originally for then an
upgrade fee may be charged.  [Your mileage may vary as may the Q's licensing
policy]


The LMF allows licenses to be keyed many ways, however the only dependancy I
have seen is for the hardware class of the machine.


Some third-party products however do some hardware checks, and in some cases
some very stupid ones.  One company that informs me that they are no longer
in the OpenVMS market because of slow sales they blamed on the declining VMS
market had the following system:  You install the product.  You run the
product with a special qualifier, and it produces a counter key and some
system information.  You then FAX this information to their TOLL fax line.
They then send you back a FAX that may contain a working key (90% of the
time) if and only if they have heard of the model of hardware you have
(their table was not complete).  Upgrading the hardware, changing the
hardware, upgrading the operating system would require the cycle to repeat
it self.  About the only change it seemed to accept without getting a new
key is the adding of a node to a cluster.  Oh, and when you changed
something that required a new key, it was not simply a matter of getting the
key, you had to deinstall the product and reinstall it, before getting the
key.

Also the information needed to be faxed could only be captured on a local
printer attached to a VT terminal.  Screen prints from a DECterm could not
be used, neither could DEFINE/USER SYS$OUTPUT.


>In article <37B742F9.C8135390@vsm.com.au>, Jeremy Begg <jeremy@vsm.com.au>
>wrote:
>>
>>VMS licences (operating system and layered products) are not "keyed" to
>>any specific machine.  The only restrictions typically applied are the
>>licence rating (units) which limits which type of system the licence can
>>be used on, and some licences are issued with the "MOD_UNITS" option
>>which limits the licence to a particular named node in a cluster.

The MOD_UNITS allows you to adjust the amount of UNITS that the key will
load.  As I understand it, this is to help in the case where a license key
may have inadvertantly been issued with the wrong units.  It also helps in
the case where you need to run the software on a backup machine.

The NO_SHARE is what limits a license key to a named node in a cluster.

-John
