Charles Gilley wrote in message ...
>Does anyone know if the VAX4300 has an external SCSI interface?


The standard configurations that I am aware of do not.
It could be ordered as one of several options at the time.
There are still orderable options to add it.

>What about the VAX4100?

Yes.
>
>If either do, what would be the appropriate 4 or 8 mm DAT to acquire
>compatible with VMS 5.5.-2?


This presumes there is a problem with running the current version of
OpenVMS, where the answers to this question is easily resolved by looking at
the S.P.D. that is off of a link from http://www.openvms.digital.com .

I seem to recall that a TLZ04 will work with VMS 5.5-2.  A newer generation
tape drive may work, or may require a patch.  I seem to recall using a TLZ06
on an Infoserver from a system.

Generally the drives that the Q has qualified mention the versions of
OpenVMS that they support the drive on in their catalogs.  They are also on
line from a link or two off of the above URL.

If the vendor of the Operating System, the drive, and the SCSI interface do
not certify that the equipment will work together, then the SCSI device may
or may not work.  In these things I have usually been "lucky" for hobby
purposes.  For a business systems, I tend to check with the official support
people for their blessings.  IIRC: The Q's pre-sale support is still a toll
free number.

I have had problems getting vendors to admit that a SCSI configuration
explicitly stated in their specifications is supported when calling in a
problem.  You can guess the difficulty in resolving problems with one that
is not.

-John.





