Charles,

Charles Gilley wrote:
> 
> I'm attempting to stockpile equipment for a long term support effort.  One
> issue I must deal with is how to *test* the equipment I purchase prior to
> placing it in storage.  I want to be able to prove to my customer that the
> equipment works, will run the system, then we warehouse it.
> 
> How exactly are the licenses keyed - machine name?  ethernet address?
> Does Compaq/Digital require purchase of a license for each VAX even though I
> will never use it?

VMS licences (operating system and layered products) are not "keyed" to
any specific machine.  The only restrictions typically applied are the
licence rating (units) which limits which type of system the licence can
be used on, and some licences are issued with the "MOD_UNITS" option
which limits the licence to a particular named node in a cluster.  Other
restrictions which may apply include a termination date (the product
will not run after that date), a release date (the licence allows you to
run the product forever, but limits the version of software you can run)
or possibly the licence might be restricted to a particular user (this
is often the case for compilers).

It is my understanding that Compaq continues the old Digital policy
which allows you to transfer a licence to another machine for temporary
use whilst the first is being repaired.  In your case it would appear
that you intend to replace faulty systems rather than repair them, in
which case you might be in breach of this condition.   However most
licences can be permanently transferred from one machine to another,
with the exception of the base VMS operating system licence which
cannot.

In practice I have found (here in Australia) that since Compaq took over
all knowledge of licence procedure has been lost: you can get hold of
new licences but don't bother asking for a transfer or upgrade!  

Regards,

