We have recently become the proud owners of a Storageworks ESA 12000
disk subsystem and are experiencing severe performance problems.

Configuration

The ESA 12000 is configured as 2 separate RA8000. Each RA8000 has two
HSG80 controllers each with 512MB of on-board cache. The HSG80s are
connected to two 7 port Fibre Channel hubs, one of each controller pair
being connected to each hub. A single Compaq 6400 Server is connected
to each hub using two KGPSA FC adapters.
The 6400 is a quad Xeon with 4 GB RAM and is running under NT 4.0
Enterprise Edition with Service Pack 5. The server uses Secure Path to
support load sharing between the two KGPSA. The ESA 12000 has one
RAID5  unit configured on the top RA8000 and a similar RAID 5 unit
configured on the bottom RA8000. Each RAID set consists of 8 x 18.2 GB
disk drives.

The Server is also configured with Microsoft SQL Server (both 6.0 and
7.0 as this system is being used to migrate a large application to the
latest version). The SQL Server database is around 35GB in size with
database devices spread evenly across both LUNs. The dump devices (from
which the database is loaded) are also spread evenly across both LUNs.

The problem is as follows:

A load of the database on the ESA 12000 took in the region of 20 hours.
This was surprising, as the same load on an ESA 10000 with a similar
configuration but only 64MB of mirrored cache on the controller, had
taken under 2 hours. When the load was running, it was noticed that the
processors in both RA8000 were constantly running at 100%. We spent 3
weeks doing test after test and changing parameter after parameter to
improve the performance. Eventually we did get the load down to 14
hours but this was no where near good enough as we could still get 4 x
this performance using JBOD.

It was decided to go back to basics. What was the difference between
configs on the well behaved ESA 10000 and the poor ESA12000. We had
proved that the Ultra SCSI and the FC behaved similarly and were
unlikely to be affecting the performance at these relatively low loads.
Therefore the main difference was the cache on the controllers. on the
ESA 10000 these had 64MB per controller mirrored across the controller
pair as opposed to 512MB per controller on the ESA12000 which was
unmirrored.

Last night we mirrored the cache on the controller pairs in both RA
8000 and re-run the Database load. The load took 1 hour 40 mins, 10 x
times faster that with the cache unmirrored. This we found extremely
confusing as we were told by Compaq that having the cache unmirrored
means that the application will be able to use the whole 1GB of cache
on the controller pair. Mirroring will mean that the application will
only use half the cache. We were also told that having more cache
should improve the applications performance or at least not degrade it.

Help!

Can anyone explain this?

