Robert Meyer wrote:

> We are using Reflections

correction: Reflection

> on

a PC to access

> an Alpha which is running VMS.

To my knowledge, WRQ does not offer any of their Reflection terminal emulation
products for W/NT-Alpha or for OpenVMS-Alpha plus DECwindows/Motif. I could be
wrong, of course. Just the same, I believe I have correctly interpreted your
meaning by inserting the above additions and correction.

> The server provides Accounts Receivable and General Ledger for multiple
> companies.  When running multiple telnet sessions to close out at end on
> month in one company from the same computer, a session will jump from and at
> the end of the report, it will appear you are now in the other telnet
> session.  What tool is recommended to monitor the IP problem in order to
> diagnose where the problem is on the server.

Well, your sentence "will jump from and" is a bit confusing.

However, I doubt that you will find any "problem" on the "server", at least as
far as TCP/IP-TELNET is concerned.

The more likely case is garbage data in the report (possibly from some field(s)
in one or more records in one of more files) which ends up commanding Reflection
to switch sessions. Reflection can be told to do many things by way of commands
received downstream within escape sequences.

Though without a more lucid explanation, even that is a guess, at best.

I'd check for this kind of a "garbage data" situation before I'd suspect
anything else, especially if the condition is that predictable and easy
repeatable/reproducible.

There are ways to tell Reflection to display control characters rather than to
interpret and act upon them. You may want to look into this as part of your
troubleshooting. Try the Help in Reflection. You can usually find it under some
topic relating to emulation or Se(t)up. In Reflection/4 V6.00 for W/95, you'll
find it under Setup -> Display and the Screen tab in that dialog box.

You can also draw the report file into one of the OpenVMS editors. Both EDT and
EVE(TPU) will display certain control characters either as some odd graphic like
a multinational character, a parity character (backwards question mark (TPU)), a
mnemonic such as <CR> for a CTRL+M or <ESC> for CTRL+[ (EDT), or a hexadecimal
representation such as <XFF> (EDT).

This may be an easier way to get to the bottom of this, if garbage data is
indeed your problem. I find EDT better for this than TPU, but that's just my
preference. Your mileage may(will) vary.

It's also dangerous to assume that anything running in W/3x, W/9x or W/NT is not
subject to Windows-related problems, as most of us here have already discovered.

