I have a good chance at pitching a VMS solution to a customer who already has
a spare VAX (used for Y2K testing and now iddle).

So, I call PMDF, get prices and tech support on the phone right away, get an
email with all sorts of documents with prices etc and their web site provides
all sorts of info as well. That is the way one expects a company to deal with
potential customers.

Now, I want to find out what Compaq's Office server would cost these days.
(formerly known as ALLIN1).

Not sure if they should make a "Indianna Jones and the price book" movie out
of this or not.

So, I got to the VMS web site, find the SPD for Office Server. So far so good.
Then, of course, you can't find prices on-line, you have to register to the
businesslink thingy. And my account that I had registered doesn't seem to work
anymore. So I register again. (third time I think).
Of course, the company that claims to be ecommerce experts can't provide
instant registration. (Why would one need to register to get list prices
anyways ?). So I wait overnight. I get an email today with confirmation of the
account creation.

I happily log in, only to find out that I don't have access to any prices :-(
;-( :-( ;-(

The adventure is only starting...

So, I call the number of the person who sent me the registration, hoping she
can quickly correct the situation. Unfortunatly, she has left for the day and
there is nobody else at Compaq who can deal with my problem.

Since they castrated the sales force, I have not had any contact with the
Digital/compaq office in Montreal so I have no clue if they still exist or
not. An expensive call to 411 reveals that there is still an office here. My
expectations are low, but I call them anyways. I explain the situation: I need
a list price for a product for which I have the part number, and I have no
idea where to go. The receptionist asks around, but nobody seems to be able to
provide me with this info, so they transfer me to an 800 number in Canada.
Person sounds like he is in Singapore.

I have to repeat the story, and the person does not understand. I tell them: I
need a LIST price. 
Person offers to refer my request to a VAR/distributor who might call me back
the next day. I need the info ASAP for a proposal that has to go out before
midnight. I tell them this is unacceptable and ask them why I am being treated
like a hacker attempting to illegally obtain list prices from Compaq.  (that
is how I felt).

I know I am not allowed to buy from Compaq, but why is it illegal for Compaq
to provide list prices to potential customers ? The person then tells me he
can't do that , it seems that Digital stuff isn't yet fully integrated into
Compaq. He offers to conference call to someone else. That someone else isn't
there. Eventually, he conference calls to a technical support person in
Kanata. By that time, the person's voice sounds like it is coming from Jupiter.

I ask: I need the LIST price for part number QM-5TB9A-SC. He gives me the
price, but there there seems to be a discussion about how, since this is ONLY
$10430, how one would go about to purchase this. I told them that would be my
customer's problem. They ask me why I don't call resellers. I tell them that
they too turn away small customers. Seems that to get any information on
"enterprise" computing, one has to be part of the Fortune 500.


Mr Capellas: instead of firing those 17000 folks NOW, why don't you get them
to revamp your web site to give anyone and everyone easy access to a list of
current products and PRICES.  Yeah, if a company has a special deal with
Compaq, let them have to negotiate a DBL and wait overnight for a Businesslink
acocunt to be created, but for J.Q.Public, give them access to the list prices INSTANTLY.

It is no wonder some of Dec's products don't sell too well. When Compaq
employees seem to think  that giving prices to potential customers is illegal,
you're bound to lose sales.

Under Mr Olson, there was something called 1-800-DIGITAL in canada
(DEC-DIRECT) where anyone could call and get list prices within seconds.
Ironic that in this day and age of instant information, Compaq now requires
all sorts of hoops and loops in order to get that same information. 

Cost ? How much employes time did I take at Compaq ? Under the DEC-DIRECT
model, it would have taken 30 seconds of employee time to respond to this very
simple request.\


It is therefore easy to conclude that there must be a very loyal VMS fan
behind any new customer that buys VMS stuff. Only an ultra loyal VMS fan would
pursue such a difficult adventure to get a single price for a piece of software.



