"Dr Ivan D Reid, muSR Facility" wrote:
> 
> In article <7ou2bg$ob5$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, Alan Greig wrote:
> 
> >The EV67 Alpha is a seriously fast processor. It's so superior to the
> >Pentium III that it must take a miracle of non-marketing by Compaq to
> >prevent end users realising this. I reckon a 667 Mhz EV67 performs
> >about the speed of a 2Ghz EV4 (and an EV4 is still what most people
> >have).
> 
>         What's the theoretical maximum integer/FP instructions per clock
> cycle for the EV67?

    The EV5 can issue up to 4/clock (2 int + 2FP)

    The EV6 can issue up to 4/clock (4 int, 2 int + 2 FP etc)

    The trick is that the EV5 could very rarely issue four instr
    and also stalled quite a lot.  The EV6 core can often issue
    four instr and stalls less because of its OOO execution and
    support of up to 80 instructions in flight at a time.


