In article <37AE7AF0.581ECD4D@hda.hydro.com>, Terje Mathisen <Terje.Mathisen@hda.hydro.com> writes:
|> Eric Smith wrote:
|> > 
|> > chuckr@picnic.mat.net (Chuck Robey) writes:
|> > > I think the average accuracy of crstals is about 1%.  Large run
|> > > crystals probably average a little better, but spec'ed accuracy
|> > > is (like I said) 1%.
|> > 
|> > No, they're generally rated to at least 100 PPM (0.01%).  Even taking
|> > temperature and aging into account, they're generally better than 200 PPM.
|> 
|> As I stated a couple of days ago, news:comp.protocols.time.ntp (use
|> Deja!) has many stories about the intrinsic accuracy and stability of
|> cpu crystals.

See also http://www.ntp.org/. Buried in the NTP FAQs, papers, and
documentation is about 15 years of experimental data which back up the
unsuitablilty of workstation clocks as standalone precision time sources.


|> While the (short-term) stability is _very_ good, the absolute accuracy
|> is not too good. NTP has a capture range of +/- 500 ppm, so if the basic
|> frequency is off by more than 0.05%, it just won't work. On most systems
|> where you can tweak the base system clock, you can do so in increments
|> of 100ppm, which is good enough to bring it into the range where ntp
|> will do the rest.

Where, adjusting the `base system clock' means adjusting the increment
added to the system's idea of time-of-day at each clock interrupt, to
correct for relatively long-term inaccuracies in the system's real-time
clock.  Which often have bad both short-term stability, and long-term
dependency on temperature. Both of which make it hard to agree with dbj's
observation about NTP giving accurate estimations of system clock rate,
unless one adds caveats about temperature and about having a good NTP
frequency-lock. (i've noticed seasonal and daily swings in non-temperature-
controlled environments).


|> I do have a Dell 410 Workstation (dual 400 MHz PII) which is off by more
|> than 500ppm though, and since it is running NT which doesn't allow any
|> kind of simple adjustment of the basic clock increment, I can't get ntp
|> to lock on.
|> 
|> Since NTP works well on other Dell machines, I'll guess that the culprit
|> it the crytal itself and not the PLL.

Have you tried disabling or removing one of the CPUs? NT is known for
having a low-precision clock, but there is at least one-documented NT apis
which give a high-res clock signal. If your NTP is using those, and NT is
interpolating time between clock interrupts based on CPU cycles, and doing
it carelessly when context-switching between CPUs on a multiprocessor,
that's known to be sufficient to give  xntpd/ntpd problems.

Jonathan Stone
