    You all know by now that AMD Athlon Ultra (the 'Xeon' K7 variety
with 2 MB++ full-speed cache) and new versions of Alpha 21264 (0.25 um,
0.18 um, copper, with 4 or 8 MB cache) use the common Slot B connector
on a 333 or 400 MHz data path co-developed by AMD and Alpha  Processor
Inc. Even the chipsets like Irongate-D4 (Dual DDR-SDRAM bus) and
Irongate-R4 (quad Direct Rambus channels) and Hotrail will be shared
among them. They also have the same 8 TB physical address space and can
operate together simultaneously.

     There is also talk about the new 'KeV' (K-series plus EV (Alpha))
technology that lets you run Alpha Windows NT with Alphas running Alpha
tasks     and Athlons running Intel tasks together. NT's thread affinity
together with Compaq FX!32 experience make this achievable.

     How many of you would like a say quad-CPU (Dual 1.2 GHz 264 plus
Dual 800 MHz Athlon Ultra or whatever combo) with dual mem buses, AGP
4X and dual 64-bit PCI? It would be big and power-guzzling, but it would
kick ass out of both 32-bit and 64-bit (Merced) Intel offerings planned
for 2000...

     Nova




