> >I was very disappointed when I timed my Intel Pentium-``100'' and found
> >that it was actually running at 99.475MHz. I was paying for 16 trillion
> >more CPU cycles per year than I was actually getting!
> 
> Yes, because a "100" was really a 33.N Mhz chip *3.
> 
> >P.S. In all seriousness: Are there some explanations for these numbers?
> >I thought crystals could be built to hit practically any frequency. Are
> >there other constraints on the exact bus speed? Why would a motherboard
> >manufacturer aim for 100.216MHz instead of 100.000MHz?
> 
> I would assume some of it is experimental error.  My system probes as
> 333 some days and 334 others.

At least in the PC environment, all current machines use a single
14.3... MHz crystal (exact frequency determined as a multiple (4X?) of a
base NTSC clock) as input into a programmable multi-PLL chip which then
generates all the other frequencies that's needed, including RAM, PCI,
AGP and CPU clocks.

These PLLs probably use limited resolution multipliers/dividers to
generate those clocks, so they will never be exactly 100/200/300 etc.

On top of this, these chips can also purposely insert a certain amount
of jitter in the cpu clock signal (spread spectrum) to reduce the
maximum emission strength.

The frequency which you then actually end up with can only be measured
against some other clock in the system, so unless you use a fairly
long-term average and use NetworkTimeProtocol to make sure that the
system clock is tied to UTC, it is hard to measure it accurately.

Besides, using NTP will also show you that the system frequencies depend
a lot upon the current crystal temperature, since most vendors tend to
use very cheap quartz crytals.

If you visit the comp.protocols.time.ntp newsgroup you can find out a
lot more about this than you probably need. :-)

Terje

