        Well, yes, StorageWorks _was_  a  big  positive factor in Compaq
    purchasing Digital.

> This sounded good. And it sounded very good for us (we used StorageWorks
> - and we ditched COMPAQ some years ago - so our investments would have been
> protected).
> 
> Now, it sounds different. Instead of making the investments of a customer
> of one or the other company (almost) obsolete, they now make both obsolete.

        The   StorageWorks   folks   were   _already_   looking   for  a
    next-generation  packaging before the Compaq take over.  There  were
    at least a couple of factors: (1) the high  rotational  speed  disks
    generate  more heat that must be disipated, and (2) those disks need
    to be more rigidly mounted (so  you  don't  get one disk doing seeks
    afecting  a  neighboring disk...yes, this is a _real_  effect).   IN
    addition, there were issues with  using  fibre  channel  connections
    directly to the "bricks", etc.

        The SW people had  come  up  with  a  form  factor that was very
    similar  to  the  existing SSB's but already incompatible  with  the
    older packaging.  When they got together  with  the  Compaq  storage
    folks,  they apparently took the best from the two companies for the
    new packaging.  I _believe_ you'll  see  shelves that hold more like
    10  or  14 (I don't remember) "bricks" with much narrower slots  for
    the bricks (one inch spacing or so?).

> I hope, the new system is really neat. My bosses are already upset enough...

        It is.  Your bosses don't need to be upset.  Your older-style SW
    shelves and bricks will continue to  be supported.  Only if you want
    to  move  to  the latest and highest performance disks +  shelves  +
    controllers + interconnect will you need to move  to  the  new  form
    factors.   For  example, the HSJ controllers we have are, after all,
    fast-narrow SCSI and will  never  do Ultra-SCSI or SCSI-III (FC-AL).
    The  newer, faster, larger disks _are_ available in fast-narrow  SSB
    versions (-VA part numbers) but won't have the preformance they have
    in the -VW or other configurations.  TNSTAAFL.

        -Ken
