John Henning wrote:
[snip]
> No new instructions were used in the SPEC submission; note that
> the submission says '-arch ev6'.  Nor were there any occult
> contributions based on the proximity to a beastly number, to the
> best of my knowledge.

   I wasn't thinking along the lines of new instructions in
   the 21264A.  There is a lot of little "rules of thumb"
   hardwired into the 21264.  For example the load hit or
   miss predictor uses a four bit counter to track the
   behaviour of recent loads.  In the 21264 this counter
   decrements by two for load misses and increments by
   one for load hits.  Maybe someone ran traces of lots of
   apps and found that, for example, decrementing by three
   for load misses gives better performance and they made
   a small logic change during the port to 0.28 um.  This
   sounds trivial but when speculative loads are squashed
   this costs two cycles and potentially eight instructions
   are pulled back into the instruction queue and then
   restarted and reissued.

   And the whole 21264 tournament branch prediction system
   looks like a gold mine for someone who wanted to tweak
   the heuristics/logic on the basis of experience with
   user code on real hardware :)

> 
