> Problem is , disaster-tolerant solutions are not only incredibly
> expensive in dollars, but you also pay for reliabilty with reduced
> performance.

If you have fiber between two buildings, I do not see why you would consider
such solutions to be incredibly expensive. I built a disaster tolerant SWIFT
system years ago. The bank had plenty of fibers (IBM had not yet announced
ability to connect DASD via fibre but was about to) and I got the use of 2
fibres. We already had the two machines. All that was needed was VAXcluster
and Volume Shadowing software licences. (and some telecom hardware at the backup-site).

For a mostly-read application, performance is increased because
volume-shadowing will split read requests across systems when one is overloaded.

With VMS, it is not that complex.



