N Novakovic <nova@pacific.net.sg> writes:

> Hi,
> 
> We connected a DU 4.0F based 21264 Alpha (with the newest big patch) to
> a X86-based Red Hat 6.0 Linux server (which also has a couple of Suns
> and NT systems connected), and the Alpha is unable to do remote NFS
> mounting of a directory on the Linux machine (using mount with the usual
> NFS switches gives the messages that 'client credentials are
> incorrect'). What could be the problem, as other systems on the network
> don't have it?
> 
> Nova
> 

Verify that the UID, GID, and permissions on the directory being exported
are such that the client system can mount it.  For example, if the server
has the following directory:

   1 drwx------   2 jdoe     jdoe         1024 Nov  3  1998 News/

If mounted by user jdoe on the client system, and jdoe has the same UID
and GID as on the server, no problem.  However, if the UID or GID of jdoe
differs between the two systems, you will get a mount error.

Other considerations:

1 You may need to specify the use of the NFSv2 protocol.  Linux does not
   yet support v3, so a conflict can arise here.

2) If you need to run setuid/setgid programs off the NFS disk, you need to
   explicitly tell NFS to allow the client system to act as root on the
   disk.

MarkG
