bryant@eisner.decus.org (Geoff Bryant) writes:

> > As to the vunerability of 3rd party IP stacks on OpenVMS,
> > there is a good chance that they were/are vunerable since
> > the denial of service attacks have had most impact on BSD
> > based IP stacks, which accounts for I think all 3rd party
> > OpenVMS stacks.
> 
> This is not a valid assumption.   TCPware is not a BSD based stack.
> Multinet is a BSD based stack, but has not been vulnerable to some
> of the attacks that impacted other BSD based stacks.  Obviously I
> am not about to delve into specifics in this topic.

	Well, I am :)

	Some of the attacks involved faking packets that would pretend
to be fragments of a bigger IP packet. When reassembled by the IP
stack, the added size of all these packets would exceed the maximum
possible size for an IP packet (64K) and memory corruption would
happen because boundary checks were not performed. A design criteria,
presumably (why bother checking, packets can't be larger than 64K per
the RFC's, and the world is full of honest people anyways).

	Fortunately, VMS' memory management is "slightly better" than
Unix'. One such attack I recall seeing while still at TGV (it might've
been after we got acquired by Cisco though) was the Ping Of
Death. MultiNet was not vulnerable to it. I don't know how other IP
stacks for VMS fared that attack.

-jav



