There are now numbers at specbench.org for the XP1000 workstation
with the 21264A processor running at 667 MHz.  This is how it
stacks up to the original XP1000 (both systems with 4 MB L2):

   CPU     Clock  SPECint95  SPECintbase95  SPECfp95  SPECbasefp95

  21264A    667     37.5        33.3          65.5       53.9
  21264     500     26.9        23.2          52.2       44.5 

  Ratio    1.33     1.39        1.43          1.25       1.21

The integer performance of the 21264A has increased supralinearly
with respect to clock rate, especially for the base score.  The
SPECint's for the 21264A were run with a newer version of the
C compiler (V6.1 vs V6.0) but it is unlikely to account for all
the difference.  So *now* can we find out exactly what are the
enhancements to the EV67 core?  :)

The SPECfp scores scaled sublinearly as would be expected since
the memory system is unchanged (AFAIK).  But remember that the
XP1000 only uses 4 "D" chips instead of the 8 found in a DS20
(in which a 21264/500 yields 58.7 SPECfp95 and 48.4 SPECbasefp95)
so the 21264A/667 might exceed 70 SPECfp95 in a DS20 or ES40.
Extrapolating to 750 MHz plus 3% for 8 "D" chips easily gives
the 43 SPECint95 figure thrown out quite a while ago for EV67.

Anyone care to predict if the first merced systems due to ship
in a year or so will be able to match this XP1000 score?   ;-)

--
Paul W. DeMone       The 801 experiment SPARCed an ARMs race of EPIC
Kanata, Ontario      proportions to put more PRECISION and POWER into
demone@mosaid.com    architectures with MIPSed results but ALPHA's well
pdemone@igs.net      that ends well.



