: 	for Out of Order Scheduling" is the culprit:

The correct title of the talk is:
   Hardware Support for Out of Order Instruction Profiling on Alpha 21264a.
Wherein the difference between "scheduling" and "profiling" is rather
important. (For one thing, hardware OOO scheduling was firmly in place for
the 21264. It may have been tweaked, but the profiling support appears to
be completely new.) For background info on this, one can see the Compaq
Continuous Profiling Infrastructure project's page at:
    http://www.research.digital.com/SRC/dcpi/

Hardware profiling support's effect on performance for SPEC would seem to
fall under "compiler improvements" in my book since it very likely
translates to profiling feedback based optimization. E.g. if this is the
case, one could take the feedback optimized executable and run it on an
original 21264 and see the improved performance.

