I run a few DEC Venturis 466 machines here, and, recently, one of the machines
decided to take its own life by letting its hard drive go in spectacular
fashion (ok then, it was just a bit of smoke, but the PCB on there is totally
buggered).

Anyways. The old hard drive was taken out, and a 130mb put in, to keep it
ticking over until a better solution could be found.

The machine didn't like it. The BIOS would recognise it. Going into the BIOS
and autodetecting it, it was fine. But the machine wouldn't boot off it. Odd.
Perhaps its just the hard drive? Lets try another one. No dice. Still nothing.

Examining the hard drive further, it tells me it's a "Non-standard" unit,
although, after posting this information in another DEC related newsgroup, I'm
told that I'm going down the wrong trail concentrating on this piece of
information.

It's also been suggested that perhaps the original hard drive had something
odd performed with the geometry settings. This is perhaps a possibility, in
that, when I put the 130mb drive into the machine, it autodetected the landing
zone as 1001, when the machine it was pulled from, as well as the drive itself
said it wanted 1000.

Another suggestion that's been thrown around is the boot sector. Perhaps DEC
did something odd with the boot sector? I'd be quick to chuck this theory out
of the window, though, considering the fact that I've installed a new boot
loader on each of the machines, thus, wiping the boot sector. (I'd hope,
anyway).

So, anyone got any ideas?

