Gary,

A couple of points....

1. There are the comp.sys.dec and comp.sys.dec.micro newsgroups for
postings like yours.

2. I can't send you an email, because your posting contains an email
address that appears not to exist.  I tried sending email earlier.

3. A response to help solve your specific problem follows.

The labelling "non-standard" on the disk drive inside the Venturis is
somewhat misleading.  DEC chose to call these drives non-standard
because they require the installer to go into the system BIOS CMOS
setup to (sometimes) manually enter in the drive parameters.  (Back in
the old old days of IBM AT computers, the BIOS offered only a short
list of standard drive types, starting with an overwhelming 10MB.)
The BIOS setup in the Pentium-class Venturis computers has an
autodetect feature wherein the BIOS interrogates the drive for its
number of cylinders, heads, and tracks.  I have not worked extensively
with the Venturis 466, but I suspect that it has a similar autodetect
feature.  

In any case, (usually) press the F2 key during the initial power-on
phase of the computer to enter the BIOS CMOS setup.  Then use arrow
keys to change the hard drive type, then reboot the system.

As far as I know, there is no reason why you should not be able to use
any IDE disk drive (at least up to 528MB) in the Venturis 466.  The
drive does not have to be a DEC non-standard drive.  DEC uses only
standard hard drives, floppy drives and CD-ROM components in all of
its Intel-based computers.  Using an IDE drive larger than 528MB may
be a bit dicey, because the personal computer industry at-large has
done a woeful job of standardizing IDE drives.  But that's another
topic... Ben Myers


