I'll hazard a guess that when you mirror the cache, the system takes
advantage of that fact to perform reliable (at least if power failure is
protected against) write-back caching on the data going to the disks (which
is a bit risky if the cache isn't mirrored, since it becomes a single point
of failure - whereas your RAID configuration protects any data actually
moved to disk).  Given the gyrations that go on populating the large b-trees
which constitute most of the guts of such a database, this could easily
improve load performance by a factor of 10.

